today we're going to look at opening  principles that everyone needs to know  when they begin playing chess and the  first thing a lot of players will tell  you different things that you need to  know about openings now while it is very  important to know some openings you want  to be familiar with some of the more  popular the Kings opening the Queen's  opening the Queen's Gambit the King's  game at the English although it's  important to know some of the common  openings it's more important to know the  opening principles behind them and those  opening principles are what we're going  to go over today even if you didn't know  any chess opening if you knew chess  principles in the opening you'd be much  better off and that's what a lot of  beginners struggle with is they memorize  these openings and have no idea why  they're moving them because they don't  know the fundamentals behind the  principles and then they end up  struggling because they're chess game is  revolves around memorization of four or  five moves when they really haven't  focused on learning how to play chess so  a couple things that you want to keep in  mind any time in the beginning of chess  you want to have a few things in the  back your head first you want to have  Center control you don't want to start  out with a move like h4 that doesn't  control the center second  you want to have King safety right now  both sides have King safety but once  place to start moving around you want to  actually have your King in a safer  position than it is now and we'll go  over what that looked like now you will  see a lot of abstract moves from a lot  of players that doesn't mean they're the  best moves you want to make sure that  you have Center control and you want to  have King safety another thing you want  to look at is you would really like to  develop your nights before Bishop's a  lot of people one of the most common  moves you'll see is pawn to e4 is the  beginning you may see pawn d5 or excuse  me pawn d4 but pawn to e4 is one of the  most common openings than you'll see  from here you'll see Knight to f3 as the  second move and you'll see this because  you normally want to open your Knights  before your bishops it's not the most  important thing in the world it's just  usually a common thing that you'll see  most people you would like to open up  your Knights before your bishops just  something to keep in mind another thing  you want to keep in mind is you don't  want to move your pawns in front of your  King more than you have to so let's say  you've developed  let's say you've developed your pieces  you've brought your Bishop to c4 you  brought your knight to f3 another King  another thing you want to think about is  you want to develop the pieces first to  the side that you're going to Castle so  if I'm going to Castle queenside it's  really not going to help me if I develop  all my pieces on my Kings side because  now I still have three pieces on my kit  on my Queen side that I have to move  before I can castle and remember Center  control and King safety so you want to  make sure that you're developing pieces  to the most people are going to Castle  kingside  so most of the time you're going to see  people develop their knight and then  they're going to develop your Bishop you  want to get your pieces center control  as you can see we have great Center  control with a pawn and our are minor  pieces and now we're going to Castle  kingside we have great Center control  and now we have King safety but we want  to be very careful because from here a  lot of players make a lot of mistakes  and they'll move a play like g4 this is  not a good play special E if we don't  have to you want to make sure that when  you have King safety you keep king  safety you don't want to move your pawns  in front of your kings if you don't have  to because it lets pieces come in here  and attack our King is a piece can sneak  in here to h3 and then get to g2 and  checkmate our King you really don't want  to do that another thing you want to do  obviously we'd like to develop our our  bishops we want to get our our excuse me  develop our pawns we want to get our  other bishops into the thing into the  action  so let's say we bring our Bishop into  the action and then our queen another  thing you want to do and that may look  something like this we bring our Bishop  to f4 again black can't move but he's  not moving yet we're just going over  what you may want your opening to look  like we move our Queen to detune we've  connected our rooks  always know that the rooks work well if  they're connected and they work even  better if there's an open file so let's  say one of your pawns were to go down on  let's say e4 you would immediately want  to put your quote your rook on the  e-file because it's an open file now the  rook has even more room to go if a rook  is blocked in he doesn't have a lot of  room to go then he's not as powerful  he's worth five pieces but if he can't  move what good is he to you so as soon  as you see an open file even if there's  a knight in the way you still want to  have your rooks on those open files  they're much much dangerous even if  they're not immediate threat later on  down the road they will not only support  your own pieces but to put a lot of  pressure on your opponent's you always  want to make sure that you can connect  your rooks early and that you have them  on open files that's very important  second thing you want to make sure that  you don't do is you don't want to move  pieces more than once early on in a game  and if you've seen the intro to the  Queens move it's similar to this it  would be the thought process of if we  start at efore and black responds d5 and  we capture and they capture with their  queen  we developer Knight to c3 now let's say  the Queen he's obviously not going to do  this he might you never know but let's  say he moves his queen to g5 I'm going  over principle here's principals again  remember you want to have principles in  your head not necessarily memorize moves  if he moves his Queen to g5 we can now  develop another piece our Knight to f3  and we're forcing the Queen to move  we're attacking he's got to move let's  say he makes a move like Queen to h6  we can now develop our pawn to d4 and we  have a discovered attack we're attacking  the Queen with our bishop let's say he  moves his Queen to f6 we can now bring  our our Bishop into the game developing  our Bishop getting him more active and  he can't take our Bishop because of our  Knight so let's say he moves his Queen  to f5 we can now develop our Bishop our  LightSquared Bishop to d3 attacking the  Queen let's say he moves his Queen to g4  attacking this pawn on g2 we can now  Castle kingside now if you look at the  setup black has only moved one piece  he's moved his Queen we have developed  all our minor pieces we have sinner  control we have King safety we can now  move our Queen connect our rooks we can  put a rook on e 1 we can control the  e-file this is going to be an extremely  strongly good game for white and black  is going to be using the next four five  six seven moves trying to develop his  pieces trying to get some sort of  counter play on the King side and at the  same time trying to get some sort of  King safety so make sure that when  you're playing you don't move your piece  more than once if you don't have to  early on so these are some of the things  you want to think about and also  this example you saw I brought my King  out early for black you don't want to  bring your queen out early if you don't  have to you want to keep him back you  want to develop your Knights first then  your bishops and then you want to have  your queen support those pieces in any  attack that you have so these are some  of the key concepts you want to think  about in opening you don't want to  memorize moves you want to know  different openings obviously but more  importantly y